Research Answers1. Microsoft Security Response Center (MSRC) Update Guide DataBase URL: [https://api.msrc.microsoft.com](https://api.msrc.microsoft.com) (OData-based REST API).Authentication Needed: Optional API key or Azure Active Directory (AAD) token (passed via query parameter api-version and headers). While an API key is technically optional, providing one secures enhanced rate limits.Response Formats: JSON and XML CVRF (Common Vulnerability Reporting Framework) documents, as well as flattened JSON lists of security updates.   Pagination: OData query parameters ($filter, $top, $skip) and cursor-like filtering by release date ranges (CurrentReleaseDate gt ... and CurrentReleaseDate lt ...).   Rate Limits: Enforced per API key/IP tier. Unauthenticated callers experience strict throttling; authenticated callers receive higher burst and daily thresholds (exact numerical limits are managed dynamically by Azure API Management and treated as UNVERIFIED specifics).Schema Mapping (CVE -> KB -> Product -> Fixed Build):Updates endpoint returns KB identifiers, release dates, and document IDs.CVRF document endpoint returns Vulnerability arrays containing CVE identifiers, CVSS score sets, ProductTree mappings, and Remediations objects mapping affected products to specific KB updates and fixed build numbers.Update Frequency: Updated monthly on Patch Tuesday (second Tuesday of each month) plus out-of-band emergency advisories as published.Terms of Use: Governed by Microsoft API Terms of Use and Microsoft Privacy Statement; redistribution of Microsoft proprietary text/binaries is strictly restricted.Known Breaking Changes (Last 12 Months): Occasional deprecation or strict enforcement of TLS 1.2/1.3 requirements, OData query parameter validation hardening, and schema evolutions in CVRF nodes.2. CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) CatalogJSON Feed URL: [https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)CSV Feed URL: [https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.csv](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.csv)Schema Fields: cveID, vendorProject, product, vulnerabilityName, dateAdded, shortDescription, requiredAction, dueDate, knownRansomwareCampaignUse, and notes.   Update Cadence: Real-time updates as vulnerabilities are confirmed and added by CISA (typically weekdays during US Eastern business hours).   Version/Date Fields for Change Detection: dateAdded (string format YYYY-MM-DD) and catalog versioning metadata in the root JSON object (catalogVersion, count).Licence/Terms: Public Domain (CC0 1.0 Universal license).Microsoft Product/KB Info: Microsoft entries specify vendorProject: Microsoft, product: Windows (or specific software), and cveID, but do not natively carry KB article numbers or fixed build numbers; correlation with MSRC data is required.3. NVD API 2.0 and CVE.org (CVE Services / cveawg) APIsNVD API 2.0 Base URL: [https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0](https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0)Endpoints for Single CVE / Bulk Sync:Single/Multiple CVEs: [https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?cveIds=CVE-YYYY-XXXX](https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?cveIds=CVE-YYYY-XXXX) (maximum 100 IDs per request).Bulk/Incremental Sync: [https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?lastModStartDate=...&lastModEndDate=](https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0?lastModStartDate=...&lastModEndDate=)... (maximum allowable date range is 120 consecutive days per query).Keys and Rate Limits:Without API key: 5 requests in a rolling 30-second window.With API key (requested free from NIST): 50 requests in a rolling 30-second window. Recommended polite scraping practice includes a 6-second delay between requests.   Key Fields: weaknesses (CWE-ID), metrics (CVSS v2, v3.1, v4.0 vector strings and base scores), configurations (CPE applicability trees and match criteria IDs), and CISA KEV enrichment fields (cisaExploitAdd, cisaActionDue).   Withdrawn/Rejected CVEs: Detected by checking vulnerability status or using the noRejected query parameter. Rejected CVE records carry vulnStatus: "Rejected" or REJECT indicators in the metadata block.   4. Winbindex (m417z/winbindex)Data Publication Layout: Published on GitHub (m417z/winbindex, branch gh-pages or static data folder). Data consists of compressed JSON files organized by binary name (e.g., ntoskrnl.exe.json, kernel32.dll.json).   Fields in JSON Records: Each binary entry includes file hash (sha256), file size, machine architecture type (machineType), PE timestamp (timestamp), virtual size (virtualSize), operating system versions where observed (windowsVersions), and associated KB package updates (kbMapping).Symbol Server URL Derivation: The download URL for a file or its symbol from Microsoft's symbol store based on Winbindex fields follows the standard symsrv convention:$$\text{[https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols/filename/timestamp](https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols/filename/timestamp)}\_\text{virtualsize/filename}$$(or fetching directly from Winbindex's VirusTotal-backed storage endpoints).Licence: GNU General Public License v3.0 (GPL-3.0).Limits/Caveats: Focuses primarily on Windows 10 and Windows 11 binaries. Relies on availability of binaries indexed through public Windows updates and VirusTotal integration; missing binaries require direct Microsoft Update extraction.5. Windows Update KB MetadataStable Public Sources: Microsoft Learn Windows Release Health pages, servicing lifecycle documentation, and the Microsoft Update Catalog web interface.   Machine-Readable Feeds: There is no official, public, machine-readable JSON/REST feed for the Microsoft Update Catalog. The Microsoft Update Catalog is an ASP.NET web application driven by proprietary SOAP/AJAX backend services. Windows release health information is available via RSS/Atom feeds or Microsoft Graph API (requires M365 admin center subscription), but raw KB-to-build mapping tables are primarily published as human-readable HTML tables on Microsoft Learn.   HTML Structure Risks: Scraping Microsoft Learn release history pages carries high breakage risk due to DOM restructuring, dynamic client-side rendering (React/AEM components), and rate limiting/WAF blocks by Microsoft edge infrastructure.6. Symbol ServerURL Scheme: [https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols](https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols)PE and PDB Retrieval Structure: Files are requested using their PE signature hash / debug directory signature:$$\text{[https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols/](https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols/)[filename]/[guid][age]/[filename]}$$or for PE binaries directly via symsrv protocol structures.Terms of Use: Intended for debugging and diagnostic purposes by developers and administrators. Redistribution of raw Microsoft symbol binaries outside allowed diagnostic contexts is prohibited.Rate Limiting & User-Agent Requirements: Standard HTTP User-Agent string identifying the debugging/analysis tool is required. While no strict API key is enforced, excessive unthrottled parallel requests will trigger HTTP 429 Too Many Requests or TCP connection resets from Azure Front Door.7. Cross-Source Identifiers & Failure ModesJoin Strategy:Start with user-selected KB (from MSRC / Winbindex KB mapping).Query MSRC API / CVRF documents for CVEs resolved by that KB.Query NVD API for CVE details (CVSS, CWE, affected CPE strings).Query Winbindex data for binaries associated with that KB and OS build number, extracting SHA-256 hashes and symbol metadata.Cross-reference CISA KEV catalog using the CVE ID to flag active exploitation.Main Failure Modes:Superseded KBs: Older cumulative updates are replaced by newer ones; fixed builds shift forward.Cumulative Updates: A single KB fixes dozens of CVEs across multiple independent binaries, making binary-to-CVE attribution ambiguous without symbol diffing.Multiple Products per CVE: A single CVE may affect Windows Client, Windows Server, and Office, requiring strict CPE filtering.Missing Fixed-Build Data: Zero-day or emergency patches sometimes omit explicit build numbers in early advisories.CVEs Fixed Without Binary Changes: Policy-only changes, registry hardening, or cloud-side fixes (e.g., Office 365 / Outlook.com) do not produce a local PE binary change.   === FILE: docs/research/gemini-kb-sources.md ===Deliverable 1: Data Source Comparison TableSourceWhat It Gives UsEndpointAuthRate LimitFormatLicence/TermsReliability RisksVerified (Yes/No, Date)MSRC Update Guide APIKB-to-CVE mappings, CVSS scores, affected products, fixed builds[https://api.msrc.microsoft.com](https://api.msrc.microsoft.com)Optional API Key / AAD TokenTiered / ThrottledJSON / XML (CVRF)Microsoft Terms of UseSchema updates, OData query filter strictnessYes, October 14, 2026CISA KEV CatalogKnown exploited status, due dates, remediation actions[https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)NoneStandard web CDN limitsJSON / CSVCC0 Public DomainLacks direct KB-to-binary mappingYes, October 14, 2026NVD API 2.0CPE applicability, CVSS vectors, CWE classification[https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0](https://services.nvd.nist.gov/rest/json/cves/2.0)Optional API Key5/30s (No key) / 50/30s (Key)JSONNIST Public Domain Data Policy120-day date range query limitsYes, October 14, 2026WinbindexWindows PE file index, SHA-256 hashes, KB mappingsGitHub (m417z/winbindex)None (GitHub Pages / Raw)GitHub / CDN limitsCompressed JSONGPL-3.0Focused primarily on Windows 10/11Yes, October 14, 2026Microsoft Learn Release HealthRelease dates, OS build numbers, servicing channels[https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/release-health/](https://learn.microsoft.c